## Why This Is Asked
Test ability to count bits with a constant-time sequence of masks and shifts, no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting
- Fixed masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F...
- No branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function popcount64(x) {
  // x is a BigInt
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 128-bit using the same pattern?
- What are the performance implications on 32-bit vs 64-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests precise understanding of bit-rotation, modulo-64 behavior, and edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Rotation via (x << s) | (x >> (64 - s)) with s = n & 63
- Safe when s = 0 using masking (64 - s) & 63
- No loops or conditionals; only shifts and ORs

## Code Example
```javascript
// JavaScript BigInt version for 64-bit rotation
function rotl64(x, n) {
  const s = BigInt(n & 63);
  const MASK = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFn;
  return ((x << s) | (x >> ((64n - s) & 63n))) & MASK;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit types?
- What compiler optimizations help ensure constants are folded efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive bit-run counts without loops, handling 128-bit arithmetic and portable popcounts.

## Key Concepts

- Detect run starts with mask m = x & ~(x << 1).
- Implement 128-bit left shift across hi/lo: (hi,lo) << 1 becomes (hi<<1 | lo>>63, lo<<1).
- Compute popcount on a 128-bit value by summing 64-bit SWAR popcounts on hi and lo.

## Code Example

```javascript
// C-like pseudocode
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 shl1(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63); r.lo = x.lo << 1; return r; }
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56; }
unsigned countRuns128(uint128 x){
  uint128 t = shl1(x);
  uint128 m; m.hi = x.hi & ~t.hi; m.lo = x.lo & ~t.lo;
  return popcount64(m.hi) + popcount64(m.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you leverage hardware intrinsics for popcount on supported platforms?
- How would you scale the approach to 256-bit or 512-bit widths?

## Why This Is Asked
