## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about data-parallel bit tricks and boundary-safe shifts without branches.

## Key Concepts

- Byte-wise (SWAR) operations across 128-bit lanes
- Constructing per-byte masks to constrain operations to byte boundaries
- Handling n in 0..7 without loops or conditionals

## Code Example

```cpp
// outline of approach (conceptual; leverages 128-bit value split into bytes)
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 perByteRotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
    uint128 M = { 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL };
    uint128 left  = { .hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (8 - n)), .lo = x.lo << n };
    uint128 right = { .hi = (x.hi >> (8 - n)), .lo = (x.lo >> (8 - n)) };
    return { .hi = (left.hi & M.hi) | (right.hi & ~M.hi),
             .lo = (left.lo & M.lo) | (right.lo & ~M.lo) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 32-bit lanes or to 256-bit types?
- What are the performance implications on modern compilers and ISAs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform cross-128-bit bit counting without loops, using portable bit-twiddling. Demonstrates knowledge of XOR for difference, and multi-word popcount without hardware intrinsics.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based difference
- SWAR-style popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining counts for 128-bit data

## Code Example

```c
unsigned distance128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  uint64_t x = a.hi ^ b.hi;
  uint64_t y = a.lo ^ b.lo;
  // SWAR popcount on x
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  // SWAR popcount on y
  y = y - ((y >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  y = (y & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((y >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  y = (y + (y >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  y = y + (y >> 8);
  y = y + (y >> 16);
  y = y + (y >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0xFF) + (unsigned)(y & 0xFF);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to arbitrary n-word widths while maintaining no-loops constraints?
- Could you optimize using compiler intrinsics if available and fall back gracefully? If so, which intrinsics and how would you keep portability?

